In the event of a blowout preventer (BOP) failure, several safety protocols are crucial to ensure a safe and timely response. 

According to API RP 54, some key considerations include:
* Using engineering controls and PPE to protect personnel from hydrocarbon or H2S vapor exposure (page 58).
* Ensuring all enclosed gas busters/separators relieve to applicable venting or flaring options (page 58).
* Installing flame arrestors on flare lines when in use (page 58).
* Testing emergency shutdown systems prior to commencing flowback operations (page 58).
* Restraining flowlines and relief lines to prevent whipping or establishing a buffer zone (page 58).
* Pressure testing equipment before use (page 58).
* Certifying/recertifying flowback iron annually using nondestructive testing (page 58).

OSHA 3918 PSM Refinery (pages 17-18) emphasizes the importance of:
* Developing and implementing written operating procedures for emergency shutdown.
* Identifying conditions that require emergency shutdown, such as failure of process equipment, loss of electrical power, or loss of containment.
* Assigning shutdown responsibility to qualified operators.

Based on available sources, the following information was found. Additional verification may be required.

