According to the available sources, specifically api_rp54_drilling_safety.pdf, the following safety protocols are relevant to a blowout preventer failure:

* A risk assessment should be performed to determine the appropriate safe location and distance from the wellbore, and appropriate safety measures for compressors (Page 55).
* Engineering controls and PPE shall be used to protect personnel from hydrocarbon or H2S vapor exposure (Page 58).
* Enclosed gas busters/separators should relieve to applicable venting or flaring options (Page 58).
* A full-opening, quick-closing valve (stopcock) should be installed at the top of the kelly to contain formation pressures in the drill string (Page 56).
* Two valves should be installed in the standpipe, one accessible on the rig floor and one at ground level below the rig floor, to control the air or gas supply to the borehole (Page 56).

Additionally, according to osha_3918_psm_refinery.pdf (Page 17), emergency shutdown procedures must identify conditions that should be recognized by personnel in all affected unit(s) as emergency conditions, including failure of process equipment, such as a blowout preventer. 

